      "passage": "<p>(1) For many students, reading is a chore rather than a pleasure. Homework, social media and video games compete for attention, and books are often the first thing to be abandoned. Yet reading regularly brings benefits that go far beyond school marks.</p><p>(2) Reading improves vocabulary and writing. When students meet new words in context, they learn not only their meaning but also how they are used. Regular readers often write more clearly and express ideas more confidently, because they have seen many examples of good writing.</p><p>(3) Reading also builds empathy. Through stories, readers experience lives different from their own and see the world from other perspectives. Studies suggest that people who read fiction are better at understanding others' emotions. This is a skill that matters in friendships, families and workplaces.</p><p>(4) The problem is that many students see reading as something they must do, not something they can enjoy. Schools sometimes focus on tests and analysis rather than on the pleasure of a good story. If students are given freedom to choose what they read, and if adults read alongside them, interest often grows.</p><p>(5) Reading is not a magic cure for every educational problem. But it is one of the simplest and most powerful habits a student can build. A few minutes a day, with a book the reader actually enjoys, can make a real difference over time.</p>",
